---
// Old & New Foundations — copy from the 9 Nov Linear ticket (ADA-69), one 70ch
// column like /problem and /hope. Each section opens on a full-bleed <Scene>;
// the photographs were chosen by the client (src/assets/story/README.md).
// Linked from the header, on its own row under the three parts (ADA-68).
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Scene from '../components/Scene.astro';
import ReadProgress from '../components/ReadProgress.astro';
import stones from '../assets/story/foundations-01-how-we-gathered.jpg';
import stage from '../assets/story/foundations-02-democracy-today.jpg';
import assembly from '../assets/story/foundations-03-assembly-movement.jpg';
import hands from '../assets/story/foundations-04-global-impact.jpg';
import valley from '../assets/story/foundations-05-how-we-fit-in.jpg';
import dawn from '../assets/story/foundations-06-spark.jpg';
---

<Layout
  title="Old & New Foundations — We The People"
  introLines={['Old & New', 'Foundations']}
>
  <PageHero lines={['Old & New', 'Foundations']} />

  <ReadProgress />
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="lead">
      <strong>We The People</strong> is founded on a simple premise. We believe in democracy.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>We believe people have the right to decide on the matters that affect them all.</li>
      <li>
        We trust that, given the right circumstances, ordinary people are more than capable
        of meeting as equals, talking with respect and making good decisions together.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <Scene
      src={stones}
      alt="A ring of standing stones on a green field, fells rising behind under a pink evening sky"
      heading="How we once gathered"
    />
    <p>
      When we think of democracy, we probably think of parliaments, prime ministers and the
      racket of elections. But its roots go much deeper than that, even beyond ancient Athens,
      where the word ‘democracy’ was first used.
    </p>
    <p>
      Across countries, cultures and centuries, people have always gathered to decide things
      together. Some think the custom goes back to the dawn of human culture itself and that
      it is our most natural way of being. Ordinary people, with no titles or special power,
      sitting down as equals to settle disputes, share what they know and agree on a way
      forward for the communities they share.
    </p>
    <p>
      This tradition is deeply rooted in Britain where over a thousand years ago, people
      would gather under great oaks or on hillsides at “moots” – assemblies where the people
      governed themselves.
    </p>

    <Scene
      src={stage}
      alt="A man seen from behind with both fists raised, in a spotlight and a shower of confetti"
      heading="Democracy today"
      focus="45% 30%"
    />
    <p>
      Democracy at its best means real participation, power, accountability and a deep sense
      of belonging to a community we create together. But the way we experience it now feels
      far removed from that promise. Only a quarter of people in this country say they are
      happy with how democracy is working (Electoral Commission, 2026).
    </p>
    <p>
      Politics has become little more than just another show for us to watch. The cast may
      change but the real power is backstage, a place we are not allowed to enter or see.
    </p>
    <p>
      We’re given our part to play, casting a vote once every half decade or so, but beyond
      that we’re just a passive audience and have no meaningful role in shaping the decisions
      that affect our everyday lives.
    </p>
    <p>This is a spectacle of democracy, not the real thing.</p>

    <Scene
      src={assembly}
      alt="A large crowd sitting together on the steps of a cathedral, listening"
      heading="The Assembly movement"
      focus="50% 60%"
    />
    <p>
      For well over a decade, a growing global movement has set about trying to change this.
      <strong>We The People</strong> is an initiative born of that movement, honouring what
      has gone before and building on its lessons.
    </p>
    <p>The engine of this movement is a gathering called an Assembly.</p>
    <p>
      Assemblies come in many forms, varying in terms of size, purpose and structure. But
      they all share the same vision: to give ordinary people a greater say in their
      government.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some are held locally and are open to anyone who wants to turn up and take part. These
      are often called <strong>People’s Assemblies</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      At the biggest scale are <strong>Citizens’ Assemblies</strong>, which focus on questions
      of national significance and have often been set up to inform the government or
      parliament.
    </p>
    <p>A Citizens’ Assembly applies the principles of jury service to politics.</p>
    <p>
      A group of 100 or more everyday people are selected by lottery. The organisers take
      care to ensure the population is reflected as a whole by factors like class, age,
      gender, race etc.
    </p>
    <p>
      The group is given a difficult problem to face, like immigration. They listen to experts
      and witnesses from all sides of the debate. Independent facilitators help to manage the
      discussion, enabling all views to be heard.
    </p>
    <p>
      The aim is to empower the participants to thoroughly examine an issue, weigh up the
      complexities, then forge informed policy recommendations that people with very different
      views can all stand behind.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because they represent the country in their make-up, the idea is that their conclusions
      also represent the country as a whole, if everyone had the chance to access the same
      information and discuss it.
    </p>

    <Scene
      src={hands}
      alt="A crowd of raised hands, in black and white"
      heading="Global impact"
      focus="45% 40%"
    />
    <p>In the last decade, Citizens’ Assemblies have been held all over the world.</p>
    <p>
      In Ireland, a citizens’ assembly helped people look at the question of legalising
      abortion. In France, whether to allow assisted dying. In the UK, a Citizens’ Assembly on
      climate change led to rare agreement between people from across the left and right of
      politics.
    </p>
    <p>Assemblies work because they feel more like real democracy.</p>
    <p>
      Everyday people drive the process so we feel far better represented than by a political
      elite.
    </p>
    <p>The structure and support built into the process make effective dialogue possible.</p>
    <p>
      And free from the pressures to win votes, funding or influence, our decisions are more
      honest and fair, and better reflect what people actually want.
    </p>
    <p>
      The hope is that these agreements are carried forward into government and policy –
      though so far, it hasn’t always worked that way.
    </p>

    <Scene
      src={valley}
      alt="Mist lying in a green valley at dawn, trees rising out of it"
      heading="How “We The People” fits in"
    />
    <p>
      Assemblies are powerful but they face 3 significant challenges before they can really
      deliver on their promise. That’s where <strong>We The People</strong> aims to help.
    </p>
    <p>
      First, almost all large scale Citizens’ Assemblies on issues of national importance are
      set up by those in power. That means politicians can limit what’s discussed and how, or
      ignore the conclusions altogether.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those in power don’t give it up easily. That’s why <strong>We The People</strong> aims to
      build a popular movement so deep and so wide that we become the ones with the power to
      decide.
    </p>
    <p>
      That brings us to the second problem: our division. When we don’t speak with a coherent,
      unified voice, politicians can choose who to listen to and who to ignore.
    </p>
    <p>
      Citizens’ Assemblies are already at risk of being sucked into the culture wars. Critics
      on the right have accused them of serving a left-wing agenda, rather than being truly
      democratic.
    </p>
    <p>
      That charge may not be baseless. Assemblies are often set up by people who care deeply
      about a particular issue, and who quietly hope for a particular outcome. However well
      run, that can shape what gets discussed and how.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>We The People</strong> is different. Our primary aim isn’t to reach any
      particular answer on immigration. Our sole agenda is to bring Britain back together and
      put power back in the hands of real people.
    </p>
    <p>
      That’s why we call our gatherings “Councils of the People” – and why anyone who’s
      actively working with us must pledge to hold their other political loyalties, viewpoints
      and policy positions secondary to achieving real democracy for us all.
    </p>

    <Scene src={dawn} alt="The sun rising over a valley full of mist, trees casting long rays" />
    <p>
      The third problem is that these ideas haven’t yet captured the public imagination.
      They’re either so small in scale that few people hear about them or confined to academic
      circles. Until that changes a popular movement can’t take hold.
    </p>
    <p><strong>We The People</strong> will light the spark to change that.</p>
    <p>
      By starting with immigration, we’re taking on an issue most people in the country have a
      heartfelt point of view about. They already have a strong motivation to take part in the
      discussion.
    </p>
    <p>
      And <strong>We The People</strong> is designed to work for everyone. Whether you have 10
      minutes, 10 hours or 10 months to commit, there are ways for you to contribute to a
      change that will benefit us all.
    </p>
    <p>
      This isn’t just for people already into politics. We’re bringing in artists,
      filmmakers, celebrities and influencers to take these ideas to everyone – including
      those who’ve never given democracy a second thought.
    </p>
    <p>
      By working towards our National Council of the People, we aim to help to create a moment
      of national cultural impact.
    </p>
    <p>
      More than just a turning point in the immigration debate, it will be the start of a new
      story about who we are and what we’re capable of.
    </p>
    <p>
      A new vision for our democracy. One that could not only change everything for our
      country, but even spark change across the world.
    </p>

    <a class="back" href="/be-part-of-it">Be Part of It →</a>
  </article>
</Layout>

<script is:inline>
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () {
    var back = document.querySelector('article.prose .back');
    if (!back) return;
    var fired = false;
    var observer = new IntersectionObserver(
      function (entries) {
        entries.forEach(function (entry) {
          if (entry.isIntersecting && !fired) {
            fired = true;
            window.posthog?.capture('foundations_article_read');
            observer.disconnect();
          }
        });
      },
      { threshold: 0.5 },
    );
    observer.observe(back);
  });
</script>
